c{a-\\mu }{\\sigma }<Z<\\frac{b-\\mu }{\\sigma })\n$$\n\nwhere $Z∼N(0,1)$ is a standard normal random variable. In our case, we have $\\mu =4$ and $\\sigma =6.$\n\nFirst, we transform $X$ into a standard normal random variable $Z$ by $z$-scoring:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(-3.2<X<2.86) & =P(\\frac{-3.2-4}{6}<Z<\\frac{2.86-4}{6}) \\\\  & =P(-1.2<Z<-0.19)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe required probability is represented by the area shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14070250/assets/1ed2c43f507a4d0ddd54126bcd3efaa2fa9b56c63ea908e8567e6959e3fc67bc.png)\n\nNow, we express the required probability in terms of $Φ(z):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(-1.2<Z<-0.19) & =P(Z<-0.19)-P(Z\\le -1.2) \\\\  & =P(Z\\le -0.19)-P(Z\\le -1.2) \\\\  & =Φ(-0.19)-Φ(-1.2)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the table, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Φ(-1.2) & =0.1151 \\\\ Φ(-0.19) & =0.4247\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(-1.2<Z<-0.19) & =Φ(-0.19)-Φ(-1.2) \\\\  & =0.4247-0.1151 \\\\  & =0.3096.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we conclude that\n\n$$\nP(-3.2<X<2.86)=0.3096.\n$$" 13194139535775 true] [17592186365641 1000 #edb/ref 17592186365642 13194139535775 true] [17592186365641 1000 #edb/ref 17592186365643 13194139535775 true] [17592186365641 1000 #edb/ref 17592186365644 13194139535775 true] [17592186365641 1000 #edb/ref 17592186365645 13194139535775 true] [17592186365641 1000 #edb/ref 17592186365646 13194139535775 true] [17592186365641 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139535775 true] [17592186365641 1041 "selection" 13194139535775 true] [17592186365641 1104 #edb/ref 17592186365642 13194139535775 true] [17592186365641 1110 #uuid "6596c70f-37a0-5144-a30f-a24837724971" 13194139535775 true] [17592186365642 1034 "0.3096" 13194139535775 true] [17592186365642 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535775 true] [17592186365642 1111 #uuid "d0c3561f-eaeb-5a5b-a5ee-8b74fd06c6e1" 13194139535775 true] [17592186365643 1034 "0